  "Number": 15,
  "Question": "Let \\(x\\) be a real number such that \\(\\sec x - \\tan x = 2.\\) What is \\(\\sec x + \\tan x?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0.1\\)",
    "B": "\\(0.2\\)",
    "C": "\\(0.3\\)",
    "D": "\\(0.4\\)",
    "E": "\\(0.5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The given difference and the requested sum form a difference-of-squares product. Since \\(\\sec x\\) and \\(\\tan x\\) are defined, \\(\\cos x\\ne0\\). Using \\(\\sec x=1/\\cos x\\), \\(\\tan x=\\sin x/\\cos x\\), and \\(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sec x-\\tan x)(\\sec x+\\tan x)\n&=\\sec^2x-\\tan^2x\\\\\n&=\\frac{1-\\sin^2x}{\\cos^2x}\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSubstituting the given value yields \\(2(\\sec x+\\tan x)=1\\). Dividing by the nonzero number \\(2\\),\n\\[\n\\sec x+\\tan x=\\frac12=0.5.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
